Among the strongest learned segmentation-based methods, differences in functional performance were comparatively small. The DINOv3 frozen decoder and the supervised U-Net were separated in Dice and IoU, but not clearly in EF MAE under paired bootstrap analysis. Likewise, the partially fine-tuned DINOv3 model with the final two blocks unfrozen remained below the supervised U-Net in overlap, while the EF MAE difference between the two methods was not clearly distinguishable from zero. The frozen BYOS decoder achieved lower EF MAE than the frozen DINOv3 decoder, whereas the DINOv3 decoder retained slightly higher segmentation overlap. Taken together, these results support the interpretation that extraction strategy choice is the dominant determinant of apparent representation quality in this setting, whereas limited backbone adaptation contributes only incremental additional benefit.
